</figure>
<p>In pseudocode, this is what the process looks like:</p>
<pre><code class=3D"hljs language-python">dfs(node):
    cloned_node =3D new GraphNode(node)
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">for</span> neighbor <span class=3D"hljs-ke=
yword">in</span> node.neighbors:
        cloned_neighbor =3D dfs(neighbor)
        cloned_node.neighbors.add(cloned_neighbor)
    <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">return</span> cloned_node
</code></pre>
<p>One more thing we should be mindful of is the possibility of cloning nod=
es that have already been cloned.</p>
<p><strong>Handling previously-cloned nodes</strong><br>
Consider node 2 in the following graph and cloned graphs. Its neighbors are=
 nodes 0, 1, and 3. Let=E2=80=99s say nodes 0 and 1 have already been clone=
d, but not node 3:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: left;"=
